      "locked": false,
      "text": "WebSockets & Socket.IO Notes (In-Depth but Concise)\n\n1. What is WebSocket?\n   - A low-level protocol that creates a persistent, full-duplex (two-way) connection between browser and server.\n   - Unlike HTTP, the connection stays open, allowing instant real-time communication.\n   - Ideal for chat, live notifications, multiplayer games, stock updates, IoT.\n   - Uses ws:// or wss:// and sends messages as text/binary frames.\n   - Raw WebSocket requires manual handling of reconnection, timeouts, heartbeats, and fallback logic.\n\n2. How WebSocket works internally:\n   - Client sends an HTTP \"Upgrade\" request.\n   - Server accepts and upgrades the connection to WebSocket.\n   - Both can now send messages anytime without waiting for requests.\n   - Stays open until explicitly closed by either side.\n\n3. What is Socket.IO?\n   - A higher-level JavaScript library built on top of WebSockets.\n   - Adds features that WebSocket alone does not provide:\n       * Automatic reconnection if internet drops\n       * Ping-pong heartbeat to detect dead connections\n       * Event-based communication using emit() and on()\n       * Broadcasting to all clients or specific \"rooms\"\n       * Load balancing support\n       * Fallback to older methods when WebSockets are not available\n   - Much easier for real-world production apps.\n\n4. How Socket.IO works internally:\n   - Tries to connect using pure WebSocket first.\n   - If WebSocket is blocked or fails, it automatically falls back to other transports.\n   - Maintains a stable and continuous connection using smart reconnection and heartbeats.\n   - Uses its own framing protocol on top of WebSocket or fallback methods.\n\n5. Socket.IO Fallback Transport Methods:\n   - Long Polling:\n       * Browser sends an HTTP request that server keeps open until it has data.\n       * After server responds, the browser immediately sends another request.\n       * Simulates real-time communication without an actual persistent connection.\n       * More overhead but works when WebSockets fail.\n   - XHR Polling:\n       * Repeated short-lived HTTP requests.\n       * Older fallback; less efficient than long polling.\n   - JSONP Polling:\n       * For very old browsers that couldn't use XHR cross-domain.\n       * Uses <script> tag trick to fetch updates.\n       * Almost obsolete today.\n\n6. Why these fallbacks exist:\n   - Some networks (schools, offices, VPNs) block WebSockets.\n   - Older browsers cannot upgrade to WebSockets.\n   - Fallbacks ensure real-time communication still works reliably.\n   - Socket.IO guarantees maximum compatibility across all environments.\n\n7. When to use what:\n   - Use WebSockets directly:\n       * When you want low-level control.\n       * When the environment is predictable (e.g., controlled internal networks).\n   - Use Socket.IO:\n       * When building production-ready real-time apps.\n       * When you need rooms, broadcasting, reconnection, fallbacks, and reliability.\n       * Best for chats, dashboards, notifications, multiplayer games, etc.\n\n8. Summary:\n   - WebSocket = raw, fast, bidirectional protocol but requires manual handling.\n   - Socket.IO = enhanced WebSockets with auto-reconnect, events, rooms, and fallbacks.\n   - Fallbacks (long polling, XHR, JSONP) allow real-time communication even when WebSockets fail.\n",
      "fontSize": 14.07045409210334,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "WebSockets & Socket.IO Notes (In-Depth but Concise)\n\n1. What is WebSocket?\n   - A low-level protocol that creates a persistent, full-duplex (two-way) connection between browser and server.\n   - Unlike HTTP, the connection stays open, allowing instant real-time communication.\n   - Ideal for chat, live notifications, multiplayer games, stock updates, IoT.\n   - Uses ws:// or wss:// and sends messages as text/binary frames.\n   - Raw WebSocket requires manual handling of reconnection, timeouts, heartbeats, and fallback logic.\n\n2. How WebSocket works internally:\n   - Client sends an HTTP \"Upgrade\" request.\n   - Server accepts and upgrades the connection to WebSocket.\n   - Both can now send messages anytime without waiting for requests.\n   - Stays open until explicitly closed by either side.\n\n3. What is Socket.IO?\n   - A higher-level JavaScript library built on top of WebSockets.\n   - Adds features that WebSocket alone does not provide:\n       * Automatic reconnection if internet drops\n       * Ping-pong heartbeat to detect dead connections\n       * Event-based communication using emit() and on()\n       * Broadcasting to all clients or specific \"rooms\"\n       * Load balancing support\n       * Fallback to older methods when WebSockets are not available\n   - Much easier for real-world production apps.\n\n4. How Socket.IO works internally:\n   - Tries to connect using pure WebSocket first.\n   - If WebSocket is blocked or fails, it automatically falls back to other transports.\n   - Maintains a stable and continuous connection using smart reconnection and heartbeats.\n   - Uses its own framing protocol on top of WebSocket or fallback methods.\n\n5. Socket.IO Fallback Transport Methods:\n   - Long Polling:\n       * Browser sends an HTTP request that server keeps open until it has data.\n       * After server responds, the browser immediately sends another request.\n       * Simulates real-time communication without an actual persistent connection.\n       * More overhead but works when WebSockets fail.\n   - XHR Polling:\n       * Repeated short-lived HTTP requests.\n       * Older fallback; less efficient than long polling.\n   - JSONP Polling:\n       * For very old browsers that couldn't use XHR cross-domain.\n       * Uses <script> tag trick to fetch updates.\n       * Almost obsolete today.\n\n6. Why these fallbacks exist:\n   - Some networks (schools, offices, VPNs) block WebSockets.\n   - Older browsers cannot upgrade to WebSockets.\n   - Fallbacks ensure real-time communication still works reliably.\n   - Socket.IO guarantees maximum compatibility across all environments.\n\n7. When to use what:\n   - Use WebSockets directly:\n       * When you want low-level control.\n       * When the environment is predictable (e.g., controlled internal networks).\n   - Use Socket.IO:\n       * When building production-ready real-time apps.\n       * When you need rooms, broadcasting, reconnection, fallbacks, and reliability.\n       * Best for chats, dashboards, notifications, multiplayer games, etc.\n\n8. Summary:\n   - WebSocket = raw, fast, bidirectional protocol but requires manual handling.\n   - Socket.IO = enhanced WebSockets with auto-reconnect, events, rooms, and fallbacks.\n   - Fallbacks (long polling, XHR, JSONP) allow real-time communication even when WebSockets fail.\n",
