      "text": "\n\n---\n\n### 1️⃣ Raw WebSocket\n\n* In WebSocket, the **protocol only defines a single type of message**: `message`.\n* Every data sent from client → server or server → client arrives as a single string or binary blob.\n* There is **no concept of event names** like `chatMessage` or `joinRoom`.\n* If you want multiple “events,” you have to **encode them yourself**, e.g., as JSON:\n\n```js\n// Sending\nws.send(JSON.stringify({ event: 'chatMessage', data: 'Hello' }));\n\n// Receiving\nws.on('message', (msg) => {\n  const parsed = JSON.parse(msg);\n  if(parsed.event === 'chatMessage') {\n    console.log('Chat:', parsed.data);\n  }\n});\n```\n\n✅ So you can simulate named events, but you must handle it manually.\n\n---\n\n### 2️⃣ Socket.IO\n\n* Socket.IO **adds a layer on top of WebSocket** (or fallback transports).\n* It **parses messages for you** and allows you to directly send/receive **named events**:\n\n```js\n// Server\nsocket.on('chatMessage', (msg) => {\n  console.log('Chat:', msg);\n});\n\n// Client\nsocket.emit('chatMessage', 'Hello');\n```\n\n* Internally, Socket.IO **wraps the data with an event name** in its own protocol.\n* That’s why you can have multiple distinct events like `'chatMessage'`, `'joinRoom'`, `'typing'` etc., without manually parsing JSON.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Key Difference\n\n| Feature        | Raw WebSocket                             | Socket.IO                           |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| Named Events   | Not built-in; must manually encode/decode | Built-in; just `socket.on('event')` |\n| Event Dispatch | All messages arrive as `message`          | Messages are parsed by event name   |\n| Ease of Use    | You handle serialization, event mapping   | Handles everything automatically    |\n\n---\n\nSo in short:\n\n**WebSocket = single “message” channel, you manually implement named events**\n**Socket.IO = multiple named events built-in, easier and cleaner**\n",
      "fontSize": 24.680594828591413,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\n---\n\n### 1️⃣ Raw WebSocket\n\n* In WebSocket, the **protocol only defines a single type of message**: `message`.\n* Every data sent from client → server or server → client arrives as a single string or binary blob.\n* There is **no concept of event names** like `chatMessage` or `joinRoom`.\n* If you want multiple “events,” you have to **encode them yourself**, e.g., as JSON:\n\n```js\n// Sending\nws.send(JSON.stringify({ event: 'chatMessage', data: 'Hello' }));\n\n// Receiving\nws.on('message', (msg) => {\n  const parsed = JSON.parse(msg);\n  if(parsed.event === 'chatMessage') {\n    console.log('Chat:', parsed.data);\n  }\n});\n```\n\n✅ So you can simulate named events, but you must handle it manually.\n\n---\n\n### 2️⃣ Socket.IO\n\n* Socket.IO **adds a layer on top of WebSocket** (or fallback transports).\n* It **parses messages for you** and allows you to directly send/receive **named events**:\n\n```js\n// Server\nsocket.on('chatMessage', (msg) => {\n  console.log('Chat:', msg);\n});\n\n// Client\nsocket.emit('chatMessage', 'Hello');\n```\n\n* Internally, Socket.IO **wraps the data with an event name** in its own protocol.\n* That’s why you can have multiple distinct events like `'chatMessage'`, `'joinRoom'`, `'typing'` etc., without manually parsing JSON.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Key Difference\n\n| Feature        | Raw WebSocket                             | Socket.IO                           |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| Named Events   | Not built-in; must manually encode/decode | Built-in; just `socket.on('event')` |\n| Event Dispatch | All messages arrive as `message`          | Messages are parsed by event name   |\n| Ease of Use    | You handle serialization, event mapping   | Handles everything automatically    |\n\n---\n\nSo in short:\n\n**WebSocket = single “message” channel, you manually implement named events**\n**Socket.IO = multiple named events built-in, easier and cleaner**\n",
