    {
      "id": "mtJ8d97nz0ktZNL2LeLTq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5181.278106444828,
      "y": 249.6959567834988,
      "width": 1381.6676025390625,
      "height": 3437.283803045476,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b00",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 467511327,
      "version": 71,
      "versionNonce": 499864351,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763928589603,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Ah, let’s break this down **clearly comparing raw WebSocket (`ws`) vs Socket.IO rooms**:\n\n---\n\n### 1️⃣ Raw WebSocket (`ws`)\n\n* `ws` = one client connection.\n* `wss.clients` = Set of all clients.\n* **No concept of “rooms” or groups**.\n* If you want to simulate rooms, you must manually manage it:\n\n```\nserver :\n\nconst rooms = {\n  room1: new Set(),\n  room2: new Set()\n};\n\nwss.on('connection', (ws) => {\n  ws.on('message', (msg) => {\n    const data = JSON.parse(msg);\n\n    if(data.type === 'joinRoom') {\n      if(!rooms[data.room]) rooms[data.room] = new Set();\n      rooms[data.room].add(ws);\n    }\n\n    if(data.type === 'message') {\n      // Send message to everyone in the room\n      rooms[data.room].forEach(client => {\n        if(client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) client.send(data.text);\n      });\n    }\n  });\n\n  ws.send(JSON.stringify({ text: 'Connected to server!' }));\n});\n\nClient : \nws.onopen = () => {\n    // Join a room\n    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'joinRoom', room: 'room1' }));\n    \n    // Send message to the room\n    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'message', room: 'room1', text: 'Hello room1!' }));\n  };\n\n  ws.onmessage = (msg) => {\n    const data = JSON.parse(msg.data);\n    console.log('Received:', data.text);\n  };\n\n```\n\n✅ So you are responsible for:\n\n1. Creating room sets\n2. Adding/removing clients\n3. Iterating to send messages\n\n---\n\n### 2️⃣ Socket.IO (`socket`)\n\n* Socket.IO **has built-in room support**.\n* You just **join a room** and emit to it:\n\n```js\nio.on('connection', (socket) => {\n  socket.join('room1');  // Join a room\n  socket.to('room1').emit('message', 'Hello room1!'); // Broadcast to room\n});\n```\n\n* Internally, Socket.IO maintains a **mapping of rooms → socket IDs**.\n* It handles all **adding/removing clients** automatically.\n* Also works with **namespaces** and **broadcasting**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Key Difference\n\n| Feature           | Raw WebSocket                                 | Socket.IO                       |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| Rooms             | No built-in support; manual management needed | Built-in; `join` & `to` methods |\n| Broadcast to room | Loop through clients manually                 | `socket.to('room').emit()`      |\n| Ease of use       | Harder, boilerplate code                      | Very simple, clean API          |\n\n---\n\n**TL;DR:**\n\n* `ws` only knows about **one connection** → no rooms\n* `wss.clients` knows **all clients** → can loop, but must manage rooms manually\n* Socket.IO automates rooms and broadcasting internally\n\n---\n",
      "fontSize": 27.776030731680613,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Ah, let’s break this down **clearly comparing raw WebSocket (`ws`) vs Socket.IO rooms**:\n\n---\n\n### 1️⃣ Raw WebSocket (`ws`)\n\n* `ws` = one client connection.\n* `wss.clients` = Set of all clients.\n* **No concept of “rooms” or groups**.\n* If you want to simulate rooms, you must manually manage it:\n\n```\nserver :\n\nconst rooms = {\n  room1: new Set(),\n  room2: new Set()\n};\n\nwss.on('connection', (ws) => {\n  ws.on('message', (msg) => {\n    const data = JSON.parse(msg);\n\n    if(data.type === 'joinRoom') {\n      if(!rooms[data.room]) rooms[data.room] = new Set();\n      rooms[data.room].add(ws);\n    }\n\n    if(data.type === 'message') {\n      // Send message to everyone in the room\n      rooms[data.room].forEach(client => {\n        if(client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) client.send(data.text);\n      });\n    }\n  });\n\n  ws.send(JSON.stringify({ text: 'Connected to server!' }));\n});\n\nClient : \nws.onopen = () => {\n    // Join a room\n    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'joinRoom', room: 'room1' }));\n    \n    // Send message to the room\n    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'message', room: 'room1', text: 'Hello room1!' }));\n  };\n\n  ws.onmessage = (msg) => {\n    const data = JSON.parse(msg.data);\n    console.log('Received:', data.text);\n  };\n\n```\n\n✅ So you are responsible for:\n\n1. Creating room sets\n2. Adding/removing clients\n3. Iterating to send messages\n\n---\n\n### 2️⃣ Socket.IO (`socket`)\n\n* Socket.IO **has built-in room support**.\n* You just **join a room** and emit to it:\n\n```js\nio.on('connection', (socket) => {\n  socket.join('room1');  // Join a room\n  socket.to('room1').emit('message', 'Hello room1!'); // Broadcast to room\n});\n```\n\n* Internally, Socket.IO maintains a **mapping of rooms → socket IDs**.\n* It handles all **adding/removing clients** automatically.\n* Also works with **namespaces** and **broadcasting**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Key Difference\n\n| Feature           | Raw WebSocket                                 | Socket.IO                       |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| Rooms             | No built-in support; manual management needed | Built-in; `join` & `to` methods |\n| Broadcast to room | Loop through clients manually                 | `socket.to('room').emit()`      |\n| Ease of use       | Harder, boilerplate code                      | Very simple, clean API          |\n\n---\n\n**TL;DR:**\n\n* `ws` only knows about **one connection** → no rooms\n* `wss.clients` knows **all clients** → can loop, but must manage rooms manually\n* Socket.IO automates rooms and broadcasting internally\n\n---\n",
